\subsection*{Before We Begin}
Before we begin, remember that an *actor* is any person or external system that interacts with the software we are describing. In agile methods, a *user story* is a short, structured sentence that captures who wants what and why. Understanding these ideas lets us create clear, user‑focused specifications. With that groundwork, we can explore how to describe what a system must do for its users.

\subsection*{Motivation}
A theater script: actors (users) follow a series of scenes (use cases) to tell a story, while a quick backstage note (user story) tells the director what a performer needs.  Defining functional requirements tells developers exactly what the system should do, preventing costly misunderstandings later.  Use cases give a step‑by‑step picture of interactions, while user stories provide a lightweight way to capture the same intent from a user’s perspective.

\subsection*{Intuition}
Think of a use case as a short play: a recognizable character (the actor) steps on stage, pursues a goal, and follows a main series of actions that lead to success, possibly with alternative twists.  A user story, by contrast, is like a sticky note left for the director: it names the performer, states the desired action, and explains the benefit, without spelling out every line of dialogue.  Both help the team agree on what the system must deliver, but at different levels of detail.

\subsection*{Formal Definition}
A **functional requirement** specifies a behavior that the system must provide to its users.  One common way to capture functional requirements is a **use case**.  A use case is a documented scenario that includes the following four essential elements:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Name/Goal}: a unique label that identifies the purpose of the scenario.
  \item \textbf{Actor}: the external person or system that initiates the interaction.
  \item \textbf{Entry condition}: the state of the system that must be true before the scenario can start.
  \item \textbf{Interaction sequence} (or main flow): the ordered steps that the actor and system perform to achieve the goal, together with any alternative or exceptional flows.
\end{enumerate}

Use cases may be linked together.  An **Include** relationship means that one use case invokes another as a mandatory step; the included use case is always executed as part of the primary scenario.  An **Extend** relationship adds optional behavior that occurs only under certain conditions, modifying or expanding the primary flow.

A **user story** is a lightweight agile artifact that captures the same information using the template “As a \textit{type of user}, I want \textit{some action} so that \textit{some benefit}.”  It does not enumerate step‑by‑step interactions or alternative flows; instead, it records who needs what and why, leaving details for later refinement.  Unlike a UI mock‑up, a use case describes behavior, not visual layout, and a user story does not contain full acceptance criteria—it simply states the desired outcome.

\subsection*{Worked Example}
\textbf{Problem statement:} Create a complete use case for an online bookstore’s “Place Order” functionality (actor: customer, goal: purchase items, entry condition: cart not empty, main flow: review cart, provide payment, confirmation). Then write an accompanying user story: “As a customer, I want to receive an email receipt after purchase so that I have proof of transaction.”

\textbf{Step 1 – Identify the four elements of the use case.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Name/Goal}: Place Order – Customer purchases selected items.
  \item \textbf{Actor}: Customer.
  \item \textbf{Entry condition}: The shopping cart contains at least one item.
  \item \textbf{Interaction sequence (main flow)}:
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 2 – Write the main flow.}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Customer selects “Proceed to Checkout” from the cart page.
